[section:namespace Configurable Namespace]

[h4 Overview]

By default every entity in Boost.Math lives in namespace `boost::math`.
Defining the macro `BOOST_MATH_NAMESPACE_COMPONENTS` relocates the whole library into a namespace of your choosing.
This is intended for libraries that embed their own copy of Boost.Math (typically in [link math_toolkit.standalone standalone mode]):
moving the copy out of `boost::math` means it can never collide with a different version of Boost.Math that the
end user also compiles or links against, whether as duplicate symbols in a static library or as an ODR violation.

    #define BOOST_MATH_NAMESPACE_COMPONENTS my_lib, math
    #include <boost/math/special_functions/gamma.hpp>

    int main()
    {
        double x = my_lib::math::tgamma(5.0);            // 24
        double y = BOOST_MATH_NAMESPACE::tgamma(5.0);    // the same function
        return x == y ? 0 : 1;
    }

Nothing changes unless the macro is defined: the default configuration produces exactly the same code as before.

[h4 Configuring the namespace]

`BOOST_MATH_NAMESPACE_COMPONENTS` is a comma separated list of between one and five identifiers, outermost first.
The default is `boost, math`. Some examples:

[table Example configurations
[[`BOOST_MATH_NAMESPACE_COMPONENTS`][Library namespace]]
[[`boost, math` (default)][`::boost::math`]]
[[`my_lib, math`][`::my_lib::math`]]
[[`_Math`][`::_Math`]]
[[`my_company, third_party, math`][`::my_company::third_party::math`]]
]

The namespace is given as a list of components rather than as a qualified name such as `my_lib::math`, because the
preprocessor cannot split a qualified name into its parts, and C++14 (the oldest standard the library supports) has no
nested namespace definitions.

The macro must be defined before the first Boost.Math header is included.
In a project with more than one translation unit, set it in the build system so that every translation unit sees the same value:

    g++ -DBOOST_MATH_NAMESPACE_COMPONENTS=my_lib,math ...
    cl /DBOOST_MATH_NAMESPACE_COMPONENTS=my_lib,math ...

    # CMake
    target_compile_definitions(my_target PRIVATE BOOST_MATH_NAMESPACE_COMPONENTS=my_lib,math)

[h4 Macros]

`<boost/math/tools/config.hpp>` derives the following macros from `BOOST_MATH_NAMESPACE_COMPONENTS`.
They are always defined, so code that uses them works with any configuration, including the default one.
Defining any of them yourself is a compile time error.

[table Derived macros
[[Macro][Default expansion][Description]]
[[`BOOST_MATH_NAMESPACE`][`boost::math`][The qualified name of the library namespace, for example `::BOOST_MATH_NAMESPACE::tgamma(x)`
or `namespace bm = BOOST_MATH_NAMESPACE;`.]]
[[`BOOST_MATH_NAMESPACE_BEGIN`][`namespace boost { namespace math {`][Opens the library namespace. Use it only at global namespace scope.]]
[[`BOOST_MATH_NAMESPACE_END`][`}}`][Closes the namespace opened by `BOOST_MATH_NAMESPACE_BEGIN`.]]
[[`BOOST_MATH_NAMESPACE_LEVELS`][`2`][The number of components, as an integer literal that can be tested with `#if`.]]
[[`BOOST_MATH_DETAIL_NAMESPACE`][`boost::math_detail`][A sibling of the library namespace, formed by appending `_detail` to the
innermost component, which holds the few internal helpers that must not be enclosed by the library namespace.
`BOOST_MATH_DETAIL_NAMESPACE_BEGIN` and `BOOST_MATH_DETAIL_NAMESPACE_END` open and close it. User code should never need these.]]
]

[h4 Code that extends the library]

Anything you declare inside the library namespace must follow it when it moves.
The usual case is the [link math_toolkit.pol_ref.error_handling_policies user_error policy], whose handlers you define
inside the policies namespace. Writing them with the macros keeps them correct for every configuration:

    BOOST_MATH_NAMESPACE_BEGIN
    namespace policies {

    template <class T>
    T user_domain_error(const char* function, const char* message, const T& val)
    {
        // ...
    }

    } // namespace policies
    BOOST_MATH_NAMESPACE_END

The same applies to anything else you previously wrote in `namespace boost { namespace math {`, such as specializations of library templates.
The convenience macros `BOOST_MATH_DECLARE_SPECIAL_FUNCTIONS` and `BOOST_MATH_DECLARE_DISTRIBUTIONS` work unchanged in any configuration.

[h4 Limitations]

* Only C++ names move. Header paths, include guards and macro names stay the same, so a single translation unit can
  contain only one configuration of the library. Different translation units, or different libraries linked into the same
  program, may each use a different namespace: that is the purpose of the feature.
* Every translation unit that shares one copy of the library must use the same `BOOST_MATH_NAMESPACE_COMPONENTS`.
* Libraries that call Boost.Math by its default name are not affected by the macro and will not find a relocated copy.
  This includes other Boost libraries that use Boost.Math internally, such as Boost.Multiprecision, so relocation is
  intended for a self contained copy of Boost.Math.
* The messages carried by exceptions and error handlers still name the function as `boost::math::...`.
* The fixed width floating point typedefs of [link math_toolkit.specified_typedefs `<boost/cstdfloat.hpp>`]
  (`boost::float64_t` and friends) are not part of `boost::math` and stay in namespace `boost`.
* The [link math_toolkit.cxx20_modules C++20 module] is built and tested with the default namespace only.

[h4 Testing]

The library's continuous integration builds every public header, plus a runtime test, with
`BOOST_MATH_NAMESPACE_COMPONENTS=my_lib,math`. It checks that nothing is left in `boost::math` and that no symbol in the
resulting binary is in namespace `boost`. See `libs/math/test/cmake_namespace_test`.

